          "explicacao": "STEP 1: Portuguese has five oral vowels (a, e, i, o, u), pronounced clearly and without the English «glide»: «a» as in «father», «i» as in «see». STEP 2: the vowels also come in a nasal version. The air goes through the nose and the vowel gets a «nasal» color, without a clear «n» or «m» sound afterwards. You write them with a tilde («ã», «õ») or with «m» or «n» after the vowel: «mãe, pão, bom, sim, bem, um, onde». STEP 3: stress. If a word has an accent mark, stress that syllable: «café, você, último». Without a mark, the stress is on the last syllable when the word ends in «r, l, z, i, u» (or a nasal ending): «falar, Brasil, feliz, aqui». In all other words it is on the second to last syllable: «casa, amigo, obrigado, tarde». STEP 4: unstressed vowels at the end of a word are weak: the final «e» sounds like a short «ee» («noite» = NOY-tchee) and the final «o» sounds like «oo» («obrigado» ends in «doo»).",
